{"team":"CLE","col":"#FF7A45","identity":"balanced","funnel":"run-funnel","plays_pg":63.2,"adot":7.3,"report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><h2>No Fixed Address</h2><p>There's no bell-cow to bank on \u2014 the lead back sees just <strong>48%</strong> of carries (league ~59%), a committee that caps any one back's ceiling and makes their rushing props matchup- and script-dependent.</p><h3>The Offensive Blueprint</h3><p>When they fall behind they abandon the run entirely \u2014 62% pass while trailing, so their pass-catchers have huge comeback-script ceilings.</p><p>By the numbers it's a struggling unit \u2014 a 36% success rate (below the 44% average).</p><p><strong>Harold Fannin Jr.</strong> is a genuine centerpiece, not an afterthought \u2014 <strong>18%</strong> of targets (league ~15%).</p><h3>In the Red Zone</h3><p>Their red-zone identity is <strong>pass-first</strong> (56% pass), which puts scoring equity on <strong>Jerry Jeudy</strong>. And with the TE commanding 18% of targets, <strong>Harold Fannin Jr.</strong> is a live red-zone option too.</p><h2>Defense \u2014 Soft on the Ground</h2><p>They surrender <strong>124 rushing</strong> and <strong>209 passing</strong> yards per game (league ~120 / ~230). The run moves against them \u2014 <strong>target opposing RB1s</strong>.</p><p>They lock down opposing tight ends \u2014 just 24 rec yds/game (vs ~35); fade receiving props there.</p><p>They lock down backs out of the backfield \u2014 just 12 rec yds/game (vs ~18); fade receiving props there.</p><p>They leak <strong>RB rushing TDs</strong> (0.59/game vs ~0.46) \u2014 an anytime-TD spot to attack.</p><p>A stingy, efficient defense \u2014 just 41% success allowed (vs ~44%); temper expectations for opposing skill players.</p><h2>The Betting Angles</h2><p>Bottom line: their pass-catchers have massive ceilings in comeback scripts (fade-material as underdogs); <strong>opposing RB1s</strong> smash on the ground and at the goal line.</p><p><em>Built on 2024\u201325 data; t